7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:29,760
He knows a lot about the science stuff, Professor Dave explains.

8
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:34,560
Earlier in this series, we learned a few separation techniques, so let's learn another.

9
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:40,080
While some techniques separate the components of a mixture by virtue of differing solubilities,

10
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:45,480
or boiling points, chromatography separates on the basis of differing polarities.

11
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:49,840
There are many different kinds of chromatography that work with different phases, but today

12
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:53,940
we will be looking at thin layer chromatography, or TLC.

13
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:59,200
This is a technique used to separate non-volatile liquids with different polarities by measuring

14
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,880
their mobility in a certain solvent on a thin layer coated with adsorbent material, such

15
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,840
as silica gel or aluminum oxide.

16
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,760
We have already learned the details regarding this process in one of the lecture-based tutorials,

17
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:17,480
so make sure to watch that video first for a conceptual understanding of this process,

18
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:21,640
as here we will focus more on how to physically perform the technique.

19
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:27,480
As we recall, all forms of chromatography utilize a mobile phase and a stationary phase.

20
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:32,400
The mobile phase moves through the stationary phase, picking up and separating the materials

21
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:33,600
in the mixture.

22
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:39,080
In TLC, the mobile phase is the solvent, while the stationary phase is the thin adsorbent

23
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:41,400
layer, also called the TLC plate.

24
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:46,760
Naturally, to perform thin layer chromatography, the main thing we need is the TLC plate.

25
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:50,480
These can be different sizes depending on how many samples we're separating.

26
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:52,160
Let's use this one today.

27
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,460
We'll also need a solvent.

28
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:58,640
The solvent systems selected will depend on the substances we're separating.

29
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:03,980
In this case, the solvent will be 95% ethyl acetate and 5% acetic acid, and this could

30
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:08,800
be modified to be more or less polar, depending on how well the components of a particular

31
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,880
mixture end up separating.

32
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:15,440
Then we need a glass container that we can use as our TLC chamber.

33
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:18,240
Of course we will also need our samples.

34
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:24,000
Today we're using two known sample standards, caffeine and aspirin, which we will label A

35
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,040
and B respectively.

36
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:31,680
We also will test a mixture of these compounds, as well as an unknown sample, to see if it

37
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:34,560
is A, B, or a mixture of the two.

38
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:38,480
This is one application of TLC, substance identification.

39
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:45,400
Lastly, we will need some tweezers, a pencil and a ruler, and lastly some capillary tubes.

40
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:49,960
These are what we will use to spot the plate with our samples.

41
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:53,720
To start, let's draw a line at the bottom of the plate with pencil.

42
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:57,480
This will have to be drawn around 1 cm from the bottom of the plate.

43
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:01,760
Any lower and your sample will get washed away when you place it in the solvent.

44
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:06,080
The silica is a very thin layer, so draw your line very gently to avoid scratching

45
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,080
it.

46
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:11,180
On the line, let's also put four dots for our four samples.

47
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:15,100
Make sure that these are far apart from each other and evenly spaced.

48
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:21,460
We can label our dots A, B, A plus B, and U for unknown.

49
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:23,780
Now it's time to load the samples.

50
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:28,260
To do this, let's use a microcapillary tube like this one.

51
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:31,260
Dip the tube into the sample and wait for a bit.

52
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:35,220
You'll see the liquid travel upwards due to capillary action.

53
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:38,500
Then take the tube and spot the plate with the sample.

54
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:43,060
On the corresponding dot, briefly tap the tip of the microcapillary tube.

55
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:47,120
Do this about three times, but make sure you do it gently so that you don't scratch

56
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:48,620
the silica layer.

57
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:53,920
You also want a small sample to maximize the resolution, do not let the sample spread out

58
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:55,320
too much.

59
00:03:55,320 --> 00:04:01,240
To spot the other samples, make sure you use a different tube to prevent cross contamination.

60
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:05,200
Also make sure that the spots do not bleed into one another, we want to make sure they

61
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:10,800
are completely separated so that the results are unambiguous and easy to read.

62
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:15,980
Now let's prepare the TLC chamber, we'll need about 5 milliliters of the solvent.

63
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:20,880
The solvent amount will vary depending on the size of the chamber, but for this small container,

64
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:22,940
5 milliliters is enough.

65
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:28,040
After adding the solvent, cap the chamber for a few minutes.

66
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:33,800
Using forceps or tweezers, let's grab our TLC plate and gently place it into the chamber

67
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:34,800
like this.

68
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:40,340
Again, it is very important that our solvent sits below the sample line on the paper,

69
00:04:40,340 --> 00:04:44,300
you don't want the solvent to touch your sample at this point, or it will wash into

70
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:47,360
solution and you can't run your plate.

71
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:51,300
After introducing the plate, immediately close the cap and wait.

72
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:55,060
As you can see, the solvent will travel up the plate, and it will carry the samples

73
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:56,300
with it.

74
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:59,660
These samples will separate depending on their polarity.

75
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:05,980
Generally, nonpolar substances move faster than polar substances, since the silicate material

76
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:12,060
presents many hydroxyl groups which polar substances can interact with, and this stickiness

77
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:17,220
that results from electrostatic interactions causes polar substances to move along the

78
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:23,280
plate more slowly than nonpolar substances, which are unable to make these interactions.

79
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:27,220
Be sure to monitor the chamber, since we don't want the solvent front to get all

80
00:05:27,220 --> 00:05:29,540
the way to the top of the plate.

81
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:33,640
Remove the plate when the solvent front is just a bit away from the top, and quickly

82
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:38,740
draw a line on the solvent front before it becomes invisible due to drying.

83
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:43,900
Once you've done this, let the solvent dry on the entire plate before analysis.

84
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:46,460
When it comes to analysis, this can vary.

85
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:50,080
Some chemicals will be colorful, and you'll be able to visibly see the separation on

86
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:51,080
the plate.

87
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:53,740
However, most compounds won't be visible.

88
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:58,980
An easy way to visualize the results is to put the plate under a UV lamp, as compounds

89
00:05:58,980 --> 00:06:03,340
with systems of pi electrons become visible under UV light.

90
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:08,020
Using this setup we'll be able to see the samples, and circle them with pencil.

91
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:12,080
If you do not have access to a safe setup like this one, be sure to use protective

92
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:16,920
eyewear when operating a UV lamp, as they can be damaging to your eyes.

93
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:21,360
Once we take the plate out of the UV setup, we now have circles that represent the final

94
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:23,560
locations of our samples.

95
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:29,460
As you can see, B traveled farther than A. The spot representing A plus B does indeed

96
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:36,440
show data from both isolated samples, and it would appear that our unknown was B, aspirin.

97
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:41,760
From these results, we can also verify that A is more polar than B, which is why it didn't

98
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:43,880
travel as far up the plate.

99
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:48,060
We can even go further with our analysis by quantifying our results.

100
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:53,400
This is done by measuring the retention fraction, or RF value for each sample.

101
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:57,760
This is calculated by taking the distance traveled by the sample, and dividing that

102
00:06:57,760 --> 00:07:04,080
by the distance traveled by the solvent, so any RF value will be some fraction of one.

103
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:08,160
We can do this by measuring the distance from the line at the bottom to the middle of the

104
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:13,740
spot, using a ruler, then measuring the distance from the bottom line to the solvent line,

105
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:16,960
and dividing the first by the second on a calculator.

106
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:23,460
RF values can then be compared to tabulated data in order to confirm their identities.

107
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:29,140
In conclusion, TLC is a quick qualitative technique to confirm whether you have the desired

108
00:07:29,140 --> 00:07:31,080
product from a reaction.

109
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:35,720
We can also use it to monitor reactions by measuring the components at different stages

110
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:41,000
to ensure completion, depending on whether starting material remains in solution.

111
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:46,500
Since solvents separate components on the plate differently, we can also use TLC to screen

112
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:51,400
for a good solvent system to be used in column chromatography, which is how we can

113
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:55,800
physically separate an entire sample using the same properties.

114
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:00,440
But for now, we have a better understanding of how to perform thin layer chromatography.
